A||||5||fiero56||exhaust leak and idle speed||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||01-23-2001||09:16 AM||||WT3 thinks my '86SE 2.8 has an exhaust leak on the front manifold. <BR>He says the exhaust smells rich(I have terrible sense of smell, so have no idea). The engine doesn't have a very solid rpm, it usually wanders within a 300rpm range, and gets terrible mileage(I think my last tank of gas I got 12mpg).<P>Is it possible/likely that this exhaust leak is screwing with the 02 sensor causing the engine to run rich, have a wandering idle, and get poor mileage?<P>And lastly, could it affect how the ECM controls the timing?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Standard||01-23-2001||02:04 PM||||Usually it's easier to hear an exhaust leak.  It will be a ticking sound coming from the firewall side of the engine, sounds like a lifter.  <P>From your description it sounds more like you need to do a tune-up on your engine.  Those are usually symptoms of worn/old sensors and ignition components.  After I gave my GT a tune-up (o2 sensor, coolant temp sensor, plugs, plug wires, dist. cap and rotor, pickup coil, ign. module, fuel filter, air filter, etc) it went from about 10 or 11mpg to it's current 20mpg.  Even with the front manifold almost cracked in half...  my idle is also pretty stable, all Fiero's seem to have an idle that wanders about + or - 50rpm.||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||01-23-2001||02:07 PM||||If the manifold is allowing air to come in, it will send the O2 lean and thus the AFR will be command richer. <P>Fix that first then check the O2 for being slow or dead.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Notchit||01-23-2001||02:55 PM||||My RPM is around 1500, normal?<P>------------------<BR>Seein Red<BR>'85 Fiero GT<BR>ICQ#: 56708070<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Outside_side2(sml2).jpg"> <BR>_________________________||||reg||4
Z||000004||fiero56||01-23-2001||03:05 PM||||I have given the engine a complete tune up. New fuel pump, oil, oil filter, fuel filter, plugs, wirs, cap, rotor, ign. module, pickup coil, ignition coil, air filter, TPS, IAC, CTS, MAP, etc. Replaced all sensors except O2, decarbed the intake, and seaeched everywhere for a vaccum leak. The exhaust leak is noticeable, I'm still determining if it's an exhaust leak or lifter. But exhaust leak makes more sense right now.<P>I'm wondering if air is getting into the exhaust through the leak, and messing with the O2 sensor. My main question is: can the O2 sensor affect the timing through the ECM.||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||01-23-2001||04:19 PM||||No, not directly (loaded question there). The O2 affects the fuel which can affect the map which affects the timing. The O2 would only affect it indirectly. If it's open loop, then the O2 affects nothing (other than keeping it in open loop).<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 01-23-2001).]||||reg||1
